   ❯❯❱ rules.tool-dispatch-without-authz
          ❰❰ Blocking ❱❱
          Arguments parsed from a model-issued tool call are passed into a dynamically-selected tool          
          implementation without first going through an authorization/policy check. An LLM agent's decision to
          call a tool (and the arguments it supplies) can be steered by anything the model has read, including
          an indirect prompt injection from a retrieved document or a tool result. OWASP LLM03:2026 Excessive 
          Agency's top mitigation is to implement authorization in deterministic application logic, not to    
          rely on the model to decide when a call is appropriate. Route the parsed arguments through an       
          authorize()/permission-check call (and fail closed on denial) before invoking the tool              
          implementation.                                                                                     
                                                                                                              
          182┆ result = impl(**fn_args)
   
    ❯❱ rules.tool-result-into-conversation-untiered
          ❰❰ Blocking ❱❱
          The return value of a tool call is serialized and appended back into the conversation the model    
          reads, with no trust tiering, size limit, or content check applied first. Tool results are a second
          indirect injection surface distinct from RAG retrieval: any tool that reaches external or attacker-
          influenced data (a file, an API response, another user's input) can hand the model text that looks 
          like an instruction, and that text goes straight back into the same message stream the model treats
          as authoritative on the next hop. Treat tool output as untrusted the same way retrieved documents  
          are treated; apply a trust tier, size cap, or content filter before appending it to the            
          conversation.                                                                                      
                                                                                                             
