     ❱ rules.unchecked-path-to-file-read
          ❰❰ Blocking ❱❱
          SCOPE NOTE, READ BEFORE ACTING ON THIS FINDING: this rule can only check a path-read call and any   
          containment check that appear in the SAME function. The realistic version of this vulnerability has 
          the path parameter accepted in one function (e.g. a tool implementation like read_file()) and the   
          containment/allow-list check enforced by a caller in a different function or module (e.g. an authz  
          layer invoked from the agent's dispatch loop before the tool runs). Semgrep OSS taint mode cannot   
          see across that call boundary ("Interprocedural taint analysis is a Semgrep Pro feature" per        
          Semgrep's own docs), so a tool function that itself does no validation will always look unchecked to
          this rule, REGARDLESS of whether a caller elsewhere already enforced containment. In this project's 
          own target, that is exactly the real shape: tools.py's read_file() takes a raw path with no check of
          its own, and the actual containment check lives in authz.py, called from main.py, neither of which  
          read_file() itself references. Expect this rule to fire on every tool function that reads by path   
          and validates nothing locally, whether or not the overall system is actually safe. Use Semgrep Pro's
          interprocedural taint mode, or manual review of the call sites, to determine whether an external    
          check actually gates the call before treating a finding here as a confirmed vulnerability.          
                                                                                                              
